    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The original equation can be rewritten as \\(x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34\\). Completing the square for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), we get:\n\\[\n(x^2 - 10x + 25) + (y^2 + 6y + 9) = -34 + 25 + 9,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0.\n\\]\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must be zero. Therefore, we have the solutions \\((x - 5) = 0\\) and \\((y + 3) = 0\\), which means \\(x = 5\\) and \\(y = -3\\). Thus, \\(x + y = 5 - 3 = 2\\). \nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).\n\nExplanation:\nReorganize the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\\) as \\((x^2 - 10x) + (y^2 + 6y) = -34\\). Complete the square to \\(x^2 - 10x + 25 + y^2 + 6y + 9 = -34 + 25 + 9\\), resulting in \\((x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0\\). Each square must be zero, implying \\(x = 5\\) and \\(y = -3\\). Therefore, \\((x + y) = (5 + (-3)) = 2\\).\n\nUsing algebraic reasoning to solve this type of problem typically involves:\n1. Rewriting the original equation in a form that is comfortable to work with.\n2. Completing the square to simplify the equation.\n3. Setting up the necessary equations based on the results of step (2).\n4. Solving those equations to find \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n5. Calculating \\(x + y\\).\n\nIn this case, it was shown that \\( (x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0 \\) only when \\(x = 5\\) and \\(y = -3\\), leading to a sum of 2 for their linearity. This problem demonstrates the power of algebraic manipulation in solvability challenges.",
